    <help><![CDATA[
@HELP_HEADER@

Binning can be run for a single sample, for a co-assembly (several samples
assembled together) or in multi-sample mode. Unless an environment is selected,
a new model is trained on the fly using self-supervised learning; with an
environment, the corresponding pre-trained model is used directly.

**Please note that there is a known issue with SemiBin2 where results may be inconsistent across runs, even with a set seed. This may cause issues with reproducibility.**
For more information, see this issue_ on their repository.

.. _issue: https://github.com/BigDataBiology/SemiBin/issues/186

Inputs
======

@HELP_INPUT_FASTA@
@HELP_INPUT_BAM@

Outputs
=======

- **Reconstructed bins**: a collection with one ``fasta`` dataset per reconstructed
  genome (the datasets are named ``SemiBin_0``, ``SemiBin_1``, ...). In multi-sample
  mode the collection holds the bins of all samples. For short reads, selecting
  *Pre-reclustering bins* in *Extra outputs* replaces it by the collections
  **Reconstructed bins before reclustering** and
  **Reconstructed bins after reclustering**; long reads always report the final
  bins only, since SemiBin does not recluster them.

The following outputs are only created if the corresponding entry is selected in
*Extra outputs*:

- **Training data** and **Split training data**: the features (``csv``) used for
  training the model on these contigs, one dataset per sample in multi-sample mode
- **Coverage** and **Coverage (split data)**: per-contig abundance tables (``csv``),
  one dataset per sample in co-assembly and multi-sample mode
- **Contigs** (multi-sample mode only): the contigs of each sample

    ]]></help>
